From db7f7530601a2f249b32730bf5dfbe9ddb586eb8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: moWerk <mo@mowerk.net>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 12:26:33 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] display: Make the always-on brightness a setting

The always-on face is shown at a level nobody chose, and which of two
wrong answers a watch gets depends on whether its ambient light sensor
answers. mce writes the low power mode level in one place, guarded on the
sensor having reported. Where it has, the LPM ambient light profile
decides, and that profile tops out at 28 percent of the panel's range on
every watch that ships no table of its own. Where the sensor never
reports, the level is never written and stays at its initial 1, which is
an always-on face that cannot be read.

display_brightness_lpm holds that level in percent, 0 meaning follow the
profile as before.

display.c sends it through the lpm brightness pipe in place of the
display brightness, so it holds in the branch where the als filter passes
its input through, which is the case with autobrightness off, and the
no-sensor guard now passes an explicit setting. That guard exists because
the filter answers 100 percent while the als is disabled, which cannot
happen for a level someone chose, and opening it is what unsticks the
watches that sat at 1.

In the filter the setting is the ceiling. The profile keeps its shape, so
a watch with a working sensor still dims its always-on face in the dark,
and the top of the profile lands where the user put it. While the setting
is unset the profile top is stored once, ahead of the als checks so that
a watch whose sensor never reports starts at the same value, and from an
idle callback because storing a value re-filters the brightness.

A port can preset it in builtin-gconf.values, which is the knob a panel
that reads badly at the shared default has lacked.

mcetool --set-lpm-brightness sets it, and mcetool prints it beside the
display brightness.

Checked on nemo, 480x480, whose als never reports: unset seeds 28 and the
panel stays at 70 of 255 as before, then 5, 10, 20, 40 and 80 percent
give 11, 24, 50, 101 and 204, every one the value
mce_xlat_int(1,100,1,255,x) predicts.
Not checked: a watch with a working sensor, where the rescale rather than
the passthrough branch runs.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate
